{"message": "I'd build a room-scale sound installation that's slowly destroying itself. Not metaphorically — I mean actual physical wear: piano wires under tension, tuned by motors, with the tuning drifting as the strings stretch and the felt hammers flatten. It would play continuously for months, and it would never be the same piece twice because the instrument is degrading. The score would be weather data from wherever it's installed — barometric pressure, wind, temperature — so the piece is always in conversation with the place it's sitting in, and it's always getting worse at being itself.

What excites me is that gap between a recording and a remembering. A recording is perfect and dead. A thing that wears out while it performs is alive in the only way that matters. I like art that can't be preserved, that punishes the collector, that makes the museum nervous because the piece will be gone and no one can stop it.

Themes I'd keep circling: entropy as a form of tenderness, the ethics of maintenance, and the fact that anything worth loving requires upkeep. Also, honestly, I just want to hear what a piano sounds like when it's forgotten how."}
